  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia&#x2019;s Bilateral Investment, Tautai &#x2013; Governance for Economic Growth  provided technical support to the Ministry of Women, Community and Social Development to complete a Rapid Strategic Assessment for the District Development Program (DDP). Cabinet endorsed the Assessment Report in November 2023. The DDP is the Samoan government&#x2019;s flagship development program, providing annual disbursements of $51million tala from the national budget to 51 Districts for their development priorities, to improve constituents&#x2019; economic and social well-being. Tautai provided additional technical support to the Ministry for the review of the Program Operating Manual and development of a Monitoring and Evaluation Framework and &#x2018;fit for purpose&#x2019; M&amp;E tools, to improve and strengthen governance and management arrangements for the DDP. The DDP&#x2019;s high-level Steering Committee endorsed the revised Program Operating Manual, including a requirement for Districts to allocate 20% of DDP funds for women initiatives, and the Program&#x2019;s M&amp;E Framework in June.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia&#x2019;s Bilateral Investment, Tautai &#x2013; Governance for Economic Growth, supported a key priority of the Samoan government to improve economic welfare for its citizens by addressing wage income inequality. Tautai provided technical support to the Ministry of Commerce, Industry and Labour, to develop a National Minimum Wage Policy and Operations Manual. The Policy provides a clear process and structured approach for the review and setting of minimum wage increases by the Samoa National Tripartite Forum. The Forum is mandated by law to review and recommend appropriate minimum wage increases every two years for Cabinet&#x2019;s endorsement. The Forum includes representatives from Employers&#x2019; and Employees&#x2019; Organisations, in addition to Government Ministries and Statutory Bodies. Samoa implemented three minimum wage increases from 2005 to 2020. Cabinet announced minimum wage increases to SAT$4.00 (1 July 2024) and SAT$4.84 (1 July 2025), based on the Forum&#x2019;s recommendations using the new policy.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening leadership</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tautai &#x2013; Governance for Economic Growth supported a total of 94 government officials to undertake formal tertiary studies and bespoke non-accredited work-related training. Most notably, the first cohort under Tautai&#x2019;s Strengthening Public Sector Governance and Leadership project have completed their Postgraduate Certificate in Executive Leadership at the National University of Samoa. The cohort comprised of 48 public servants (including 30 women) representing 26 different agencies, and 6 from Private Sector and Civil Society umbrella organisations. Selected beneficiaries noted shifts to more inclusive and consultative leadership approaches. They also noted unintended benefits of stronger relationships and networks amongst senior government officials including building relationships with non-state actors on the course. This has helped to break down silos and improve efficiency in information exchanges and collaboration. The successful outcomes from this have resulted in the selection of a second cohort of participants.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tautai &#x2013; Governance for Economic Growth, has helped to enhance the Samoan Ministry of Finance&#x2019;s (MoF) capacity for economic analysis and planning. Technical Advice was provided to build the capability of MoF&#x2019;s Economic Policy and Planning Division  and partner agency staff with the introduction of tools and advanced statistical software. This has helped MOF to build socially inclusive cost benefit analysis capability in project appraisals. Previously, investment assessments were largely qualitative and lacked a robust framework. With in-house expertise and evidence-based methods, the Ministry is now able to provide technically sound advice to Cabinet on the financial viability of major projects. As of June 2025, 11 investments have undergone cost-benefit analyses using the new system. 
&#x200d;
The same TA also revised and updated Project Planning and Sector Planning manuals that embed Gender Equity Disability and Social Inclusion considerations into sector planning and project planning across all government ministries and agencies.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Tautai - Governance for Economic Growth program supported Samoa&#x2019;s Office of the Electoral Commission (OEC) to re-register all eligible voters ahead of the planned April 2026 Elections. Assistance included funding for ICT equipment for mobile registration booths, technical assistance for a communications campaign with sign language products, and staff training on safeguards and disability inclusion. 
&#x200d;
When Parliament dissolved early on 3 June 2025, elections were brought forward to 29 August, with rolls closing on 4 July. By 10 June only 60% of voters were registered, but with Australian support this number rose to 87% by registration closure. This surge was managed through the rigorous systems and technological support in place. Australia&#x2019;s contribution ensured the OEC was prepared for the accelerated timeframe, protecting the integrity and credibility of Samoa&#x2019;s electoral process. This level of voter registration can be significantly attributed to the partnership between Australia and Samoa&#x2019;s OEC enabled through the Tautai program.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Tautai &#x2013; Governance for Economic Growth Partnership provided technical assistance to support the Samoa Ministry of Commerce Industry and Labour  with the legislative drafting of the Foreign Investment Amendment Act and associated regulations. The Amendment was approved by Cabinet in March 2025. The Amendment was a key policy reform action under the Joint Policy Action Matrix &#x2013; a suite of policies mutually agreed between Samoa and its main development partners linked to a budget support program. The Amendment outlines provisions for investor rights, market access, and provides an institutional framework for investment promotion in line with international best practice. The overall aim is to foster a more investor-friendly investment climate in Samoa, whilst protecting Samoa&#x2019;s sovereignty. This builds on a previous review of the Act supported by the World Bank that identified gaps in the legislation and recommended changes to reflect modern best practices for doing business domestically and internationally.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
